      {
        "label": "International rules",
        "text": "Argued that the same principles must apply in the South China Sea, Taiwan Strait, Middle East, Sudan, and Democratic Republic of the Congo, with particular responsibility falling on powerful states.",
        "evidence": [
          {
            "sentence_id": "S012",
            "quote": "This is why we need rules, not because all states are equally powerful, but precisely because they are not."
          },
          {
            "sentence_id": "S013",
            "quote": "Rules have value only if they bind everyone, especially those powerful enough to break them."
          },
          {
            "sentence_id": "S014",
            "quote": "When rules are cast aside, raw power fills the vacuum."
          },
          {
            "sentence_id": "S015",
            "quote": "A world dominated by great-power competition is a volatile world, less able to address the most pressing challenges of our time."
          },
          {
            "sentence_id": "S016",
            "quote": "It is a world where a single spark can ignite a fire."
          },
          {
            "sentence_id": "S017",
            "quote": "Instead, we should insist that with greater power comes greater responsibility: responsibility in the use of force, responsibility to exercise restraint, but also responsibility to use one’s influence to prevent force from becoming necessary in the first place."
